/* The Grounded Readings Library, group east2 (GWG BLD 798). Written by build-readings.py; edit the data, then build again. */
(window.GGReadingsData = window.GGReadingsData || {})["east2"] = [{"id":"east-gitanjali-61","title":"The Sleep That Flits on Baby’s Eyes, Gitanjali 61","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["birth","blessing","evening"],"text":"The sleep that flits on baby's eyes, does anybody know from where it comes? Yes, there is a rumour that it has its dwelling there, in the fairy village among shadows of the forest dimly lit with glow-worms, there hang two timid buds of enchantment. From there it comes to kiss baby's eyes.\n\nThe smile that flickers on baby's lips when he sleeps, does anybody know where it was born? Yes, there is a rumour that a young pale beam of a crescent moon touched the edge of a vanishing autumn cloud, and there the smile was first born in the dream of a dew-washed morning, the smile that flickers on baby's lips when he sleeps.\n\nThe sweet, soft freshness that blooms on baby's limbs, does anybody know where it was hidden so long? Yes, when the mother was a young girl it lay pervading her heart in tender and silent mystery of love, the sweet, soft freshness that has bloomed on baby's limbs.","note":"A lullaby blessing for a baby or a newborn’s naming.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-67","title":"Thou Art the Sky and Thou Art the Nest, Gitanjali 67","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["home","peace","evening"],"text":"Thou art the sky and thou art the nest as well.\n\nO thou beautiful, there in the nest is thy love that encloses the soul with colours and sounds and odours.\n\nThere comes the morning with the golden basket in her right hand bearing the wreath of beauty, silently to crown the earth.\n\nAnd there comes the evening over the lonely meadows deserted by herds, through trackless paths, carrying cool draughts of peace in her golden pitcher from the western ocean of rest.\n\nBut there, where spreads the infinite sky for the soul to take her flight in, reigns the stainless white radiance. There is no day nor night, nor form nor colour, and never, never a word.","note":"A reading on God as both the wide sky and the safe nest, for a home blessing.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-11","title":"Leave This Chanting and Singing, Gitanjali 11","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["daily","home","healing"],"text":"Leave this chanting and singing and telling of beads! Whom dost thou worship in this lonely dark corner of a temple with doors all shut? Open thine eyes and see thy God is not before thee!\n\nHe is there where the tiller is tilling the hard ground and where the pathmaker is breaking stones. He is with them in sun and in shower, and his garment is covered with dust. Put of thy holy mantle and even like him come down on the dusty soil!\n\nDeliverance? Where is this deliverance to be found? Our master himself has joyfully taken upon him the bonds of creation; he is bound with us all for ever.\n\nCome out of thy meditations and leave aside thy flowers and incense! What harm is there if thy clothes become tattered and stained? Meet him and stand by him in toil and in sweat of thy brow.","note":"A call to find God among people who work the land and break stones, for service and workers’ blessings.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-carus-sick","title":"To Befriend the Poor and Nourish the Afflicted","by":"Traditional, compiled by Paul Carus","year":1894,"trad":["buddhist"],"type":"story","occasion":["healing","comfort"],"text":"An old bhikkhu of a surly disposition was afflicted with a loathsome disease the sight and smell of which was so nauseating that no one would come near him or help him in his distress. And it happened that the World-honored One came to the vihāra in which the unfortunate man lay; hearing of the case he ordered warm water to be prepared and went to the sick-room to administer unto the sores of the patient with his own hand, saying to his disciples:\n\n\"The Tathāgata has come into the world to befriend the poor, to succor the unprotected, to nourish those in bodily affliction, both the followers of the Dharma and unbelievers, to give sight to the blind and enlighten the minds of the deluded, to stand up for the rights of orphans as well as the aged, and in so doing to set an example to others. This is the consummation of his work, and thus he attains the great goal of life as the rivers that lose themselves in the ocean.\"","note":"The Buddha washes the sores of a sick monk no one would tend; a reading for caregivers, nurses, and chaplains.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Gospel of Buddha, compiled by Paul Carus (1894)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/35895"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1894; Carus died 1919","denom":["theravada"]},{"id":"east-shinran-paradise","title":"Myriads of Happy Souls, Shinran","by":"Shinran Shonin","tr":"S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck","year":1921,"trad":["buddhist"],"denom":["pureland","mahayana"],"type":"chant","occasion":["funeral","comfort","dying"],"text":"Whoso would be born into Paradise shall in this life be made one with those men that return no more unto birth and death.\n\nIn that Pure Land is none who hath stood among doubting men, and none also who hath trusted in his own deeds for Salvation. To this do all the Buddhas witness.\n\nIf all having life in the Ten Regions hear this Holiest Name of Him that is Infinite, and attain unto the true faith, they shall obtain joy and gladness.\n\nMyriads of happy souls were born, are born and shall be born into that Land of Purity, not from this world alone, but from the hidden worlds also, and the Ten Regions.","note":"Pure Land psalms of the Land of Peace, read at Jodo Shinshu funerals and memorials.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Buddhist Psalms of Shinran Shonin, tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck (1921)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7015"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1921"},{"id":"east-ttc-7","title":"Heaven Is Long-Enduring, Tao Te Ching 7","by":"Lao Tzu","tr":"James Legge","year":1891,"trad":["taoist"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["funeral","celebration"],"text":"Heaven is long-enduring and earth continues long. The reason why heaven and earth are able to endure and continue thus long is because they do not live of, or for, themselves. This is how they are able to continue and endure.\n\nTherefore the sage puts his own person last, and yet it is found in the foremost place; he treats his person as if it were foreign to him, and yet that person is preserved. Is it not because he has no personal and private ends, that therefore such ends are realised?","note":"On the sage who puts self last and so is found in front; fitting for a selfless life.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Tao Teh King, tr. James Legge, Sacred Books of the East vol. 39 (1891)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/216"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1891; Legge died 1897","denom":[]},{"id":"east-ttc-9","title":"When the Work Is Done, Tao Te Ching 9","by":"Lao Tzu","tr":"James Legge","year":1891,"trad":["taoist"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["celebration","daily"],"text":"It is better to leave a vessel unfilled, than to attempt to carry it when it is full. If you keep feeling a point that has been sharpened, the point cannot long preserve its sharpness.\n\nWhen gold and jade fill the hall, their possessor cannot keep them safe. When wealth and honours lead to arrogancy, this brings its evil on itself. When the work is done, and one's name is becoming distinguished, to withdraw into obscurity is the way of Heaven.","note":"When the work is done and one’s name is becoming known, to withdraw is the way of Heaven. For retirements.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Tao Teh King, tr. James Legge, Sacred Books of the East vol. 39 (1891)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/216"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1891; Legge died 1897","denom":[]},{"id":"east-kabir-88","title":"This Day Is Dear to Me Above All Other Days, Kabir","by":"Kabir","tr":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1915,"trad":["hindu","sikh","sbnr"],"denom":["bhakti"],"type":"poem","occasion":["celebration","home","wedding"],"text":"This day is dear to me above all other days, for to-day the Beloved Lord is a guest in my house;\nMy chamber and my courtyard are beautiful with His presence.\nMy longings sing His Name, and they are become lost in His great beauty:\nI wash His feet, and I look upon His Face; and I lay before Him as an offering my body, my mind, and all that I have.\nWhat a day of gladness is that day in which my Beloved, who is my treasure, comes to my house!\nAll evils fly from my heart when I see my Lord.\n\"My love has touched Him; my heart is longing for the Name which is Truth.\"\nThus sings Kabir, the servant of all servants.","translit":"aj din ke main jaun balihari","note":"A song of joy when the Beloved is a guest in the house; for house blessings and festive days.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Songs of Kabir, tr. Rabindranath Tagore (1915)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6519"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1915; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-kabir-94","title":"My Land Is a Sorrowless Land, Kabir","by":"Kabir","tr":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1915,"trad":["hindu","sikh","sbnr"],"denom":["bhakti"],"type":"poem","occasion":["dying","comfort","funeral"],"text":"O sadhu! my land is a sorrowless land.\nI cry aloud to all, to the king and the beggar, the emperor and the fakir,\nWhosoever seeks for shelter in the Highest, let all come and settle in my land!\nLet the weary come and lay his burdens here!\n\nSo live here, my brother, that you may cross with ease to that other shore.\nIt is a land without earth or sky, without moon or stars;\nFor only the radiance of Truth shines in my Lord's Durbar.\nKabir says: \"O beloved brother! naught is essential save Truth.\"","translit":"avadhu begam des' hamara","note":"Kabir invites the weary to lay down their burdens in a land beyond sorrow.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Songs of Kabir, tr. Rabindranath Tagore (1915)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6519"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1915; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-kabir-30","title":"On This Tree Is a Bird, Kabir","by":"Kabir","tr":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1915,"trad":["hindu","sikh","sbnr"],"denom":["bhakti"],"type":"poem","occasion":["dying","nature","funeral"],"text":"On this tree is a bird: it dances in the joy of life. None knows where it is: and who knows what the burden of its music may be?\nWhere the branches throw a deep shade, there does it have its nest: and it comes in the evening and flies away in the morning, and says not a word of that which it means.\nNone tell me of this bird that sings within me.\nIt is neither coloured nor colourless: it has neither form nor outline:\nIt sits in the shadow of love.\nIt dwells within the Unattainable, the Infinite, and the Eternal; and no one marks when it comes and goes.\nKabir says: \"O brother Sadhu! deep is the mystery. Let wise men seek to know where rests that bird.\"","translit":"ya tarvar men ek pakheru","note":"A song of the soul as a bird that comes in the evening and flies away in the morning.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Songs of Kabir, tr. Rabindranath Tagore (1915)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6519"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1915; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-fruit-gathering-75","title":"Naked and Nameless, Fruit-Gathering 75","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1916,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["birth","celebration","dying","gratitude"],"text":"It was only the other day that I came to your earth, naked and nameless, with a wailing cry.\n\nTo-day my voice is glad, while you, my lord, stand aside to make room that I may fill my life.\n\nEven when I bring you my songs for an offering I have the secret hope that men will come and love me for them.\n\nYou love to discover that I love this world where you have brought me.","note":"A reading on the journey from birth to a glad farewell.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Fruit-Gathering, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1916)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6522"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1916; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-fruit-gathering-72","title":"The Joy Ran from All the World to Build My Body, Fruit-Gathering 72","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1916,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["birth","blessing","gratitude","celebration"],"text":"The joy ran from all the world to build my body.\n\nThe lights of the skies kissed and kissed her till she woke.\n\nFlowers of hurrying summers sighed in her breath and voices of winds and water sang in her movements.\n\nThe passion of the tide of colours in clouds and in forests flowed into her life, and the music of all things caressed her limbs into shape.\n\nShe is my bride, she has lighted her lamp in my house.","note":"A joyful poem for a new baby or a birthday blessing.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Fruit-Gathering, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1916)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6522"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1916; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-jain-namokar","title":"The Namokar Mantra","by":"Traditional Jain prayer","year":2026,"trad":["jain"],"denom":["shvetambara","digambara"],"type":"chant","occasion":["daily","dying","blessing","morning"],"text":"I bow to the Arihants, who have conquered every inner enemy.\nI bow to the Siddhas, the souls set free.\nI bow to the Acharyas, the spiritual leaders.\nI bow to the Upadhyayas, the teachers.\nI bow to all the holy women and men of the world.\n\nThis fivefold bow clears away every wrong.\nOf all blessings, it is the first and the highest.","translit":"Namo Arihantanam, Namo Siddhanam, Namo Ayariyanam, Namo Uvajjhayanam, Namo Loe Savva Sahunam","note":"The most sacred Jain prayer, said on waking, before every undertaking, and softly at the bedside of the dying.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"traditional Prakrit text with no known author; the English is Grounded’s plain rendering"},{"id":"east-jain-khamemi","title":"I Forgive All Living Beings","by":"Traditional Jain prayer","year":2026,"trad":["jain"],"denom":["shvetambara","digambara"],"type":"prayer","occasion":["forgiveness","peace","dying"],"text":"I forgive all living beings.\nMay all living beings forgive me.\nI am a friend to every being,\nand I hold enmity toward none.","translit":"Khamemi savve jiva, savve jiva khamantu me, mitti me savva bhuesu, veram majjham na kenai","note":"The Khamemi Savve Jiva, said at the close of Paryushan and on Forgiveness Day, and fitting at the end of life.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"traditional Prakrit text with no known author; the English is Grounded’s plain rendering"},{"id":"east-jain-every-life","title":"A Blessing for Every Life","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["jain"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["daily","nature","peace"],"text":"May my steps be gentle on the earth.\nMay my words be kind and true.\nMay I take only what I need,\nand leave every creature to its own good life.\nMay friendship reach every living soul.","note":"A Grounded blessing in the spirit of ahimsa, gentleness toward all life.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-jain-michhami","title":"Michhami Dukkadam: A Blessing for Forgiveness Day","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["jain"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["forgiveness","season","home"],"text":"Today we make things right.\nTo everyone we have hurt, in thought, word, or deed,\nknowingly or unknowingly,\nwe ask forgiveness with open hands.\nWe begin the year light and clear,\nfriends to all.","note":"A Grounded blessing for families on Samvatsari, the Jain day of forgiveness. Michhami dukkadam is the traditional greeting.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-jain-peaceful-passing","title":"For a Peaceful Passing, in the Jain Spirit","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["jain"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["dying","comfort"],"text":"May your soul be calm and clear.\nMay every bond be released in peace.\nMay you rest in the Namokar,\nheld by the love of those beside you,\nand rise toward the light of your own true self.","note":"A Grounded blessing for families keeping watch, alongside the Namokar Mantra and their own prayers.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hmong-ancestors","title":"For the Ancestors Who Walk Before Us","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hmong"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["funeral","grief","home"],"text":"We remember the grandmothers and grandfathers\nwho carried us across mountains and rivers.\nTheir courage lives in our hands.\nTheir love lives in our children.\nWe honor them, and we carry their names forward.","note":"A Grounded blessing to honor Hmong elders and ancestors. Words by Grow With Grounded, written to honor this tradition; families keep their own prayers and ceremonies, led by their own elders.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hmong-new-baby","title":"Welcome, Little One","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hmong"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["birth","blessing","home"],"text":"Welcome, little one, to this family and this clan.\nYour name is a gift.\nYour grandparents' hopes are tied around you like soft threads.\nGrow strong, grow kind,\nand know that you belong here.","note":"A Grounded welcome for a new baby, offered with respect for the family’s own naming and string-tying ceremony. Words by Grow With Grounded, written to honor this tradition; families keep their own prayers and ceremonies, led by their own elders.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hmong-journey","title":"For the Long Journey Home","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hmong"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["dying","funeral","comfort"],"text":"Go gently, beloved one.\nYour family is gathered.\nYour ancestors are waiting with open arms.\nWe will keep your stories, your songs, and your love.\nTravel in peace to the place where you belong.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a Hmong family at a loved one’s passing. The family’s own funeral rites, songs, and guides lead the way. Words by Grow With Grounded, written to honor this tradition; families keep their own prayers and ceremonies, led by their own elders.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hmong-new-year","title":"For the New Year Harvest","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hmong"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","gratitude","celebration"],"text":"The harvest is in. The family is together.\nWe give thanks for rice and rain, for strong backs and steady hearts.\nWe honor those who came before\nand welcome the year ahead with good wishes for everyone.","note":"A Grounded gratitude blessing for Hmong New Year gatherings. Words by Grow With Grounded, written to honor this tradition; families keep their own prayers and ceremonies, led by their own elders.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hmong-elders","title":"For Our Elders","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hmong"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["gratitude","home","healing"],"text":"Thank you, grandmother. Thank you, grandfather.\nYou kept the stories when the road was hard.\nMay your days be warm and your table full.\nMay your children's children sit at your feet and learn.","note":"A Grounded blessing to honor Hmong elders at a gathering or a birthday. Words by Grow With Grounded, written to honor this tradition; families keep their own prayers and ceremonies, led by their own elders.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hmong-home","title":"For a Family Home","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hmong"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["home","blessing"],"text":"May this house be full of laughter and good food.\nMay the family gather here in every season.\nMay the ancestors be honored and the children be safe.\nMay everyone who enters leave with a full heart.","note":"A Grounded home blessing in honor of Hmong family life. Words by Grow With Grounded, written to honor this tradition; families keep their own prayers and ceremonies, led by their own elders.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-indigenous-honor-land","title":"For the Land We Stand On","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["indigenous","nature"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["nature","gratitude","daily"],"text":"We give thanks for this land\nand for the peoples who have cared for it from the beginning.\nWe honor their names, their languages, and their ways.\nMay we walk here with respect,\nand leave it whole for the generations to come.","note":"Grounded words of thanks and respect, suitable before a gathering. Native nations keep their own prayers and ceremonies, led by their own people.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-indigenous-all-relatives","title":"For All Our Relatives","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["indigenous","nature","all"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["nature","peace","gratitude"],"text":"We are thankful for the earth that holds us,\nthe water that gives us life,\nthe green and growing things,\nthe four-legged, the winged, and the swimming ones,\nand every person in this circle.\nWe are all related.","note":"A Grounded blessing in honor of the kinship with all creation that Eastman and Zitkala-Sa wrote about; it speaks in Grounded’s own words.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-indigenous-elder-passing","title":"For an Elder Gone Ahead","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["indigenous"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["funeral","grief","dying"],"text":"You walked a long and good road.\nYou carried the stories and the wisdom.\nNow you go ahead of us, and we will follow in our time.\nWe will speak your name with love\nand teach the young what you taught us.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a family honoring an elder. The family’s own spiritual leaders guide the funeral and its ceremonies.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-indigenous-new-life","title":"For a New Life in the Circle","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["indigenous"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["birth","blessing"],"text":"A new life has come into the circle.\nThe grandparents smile. The ancestors rejoice.\nMay this child grow strong in body, mind, and spirit,\nknow who their people are,\nand walk in a good way all their days.","note":"A Grounded welcome for a baby, offered with respect for the family’s own naming traditions.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-african-ancestors","title":"We Call Your Names","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["african","blackchurch"],"type":"litany","occasion":["funeral","grief","celebration"],"text":"We remember those who came before us.\nWe call your names, and you are here.\nYour strength runs in our blood.\nYour songs live in our mouths.\nYour hope lights our way.\nWe stand on your shoulders, and we rise.","note":"A Grounded remembrance for families honoring ancestors; each family may add the names of their own beloved dead.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-african-libation","title":"A Water Remembrance","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["african"],"type":"litany","occasion":["funeral","celebration","grief"],"text":"Water is life. We pour it in remembrance.\nFor the elders who taught us, we pour.\nFor those who crossed the waters, we pour.\nFor those whose names are known, and those whose names are known only to God, we pour.\nFor the children not yet born, we pour.\nMay the circle be unbroken.","note":"A Grounded remembrance with clear water, inspired by the African and diaspora practice of libation; families with their own tradition use their own words.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-african-village","title":"It Takes All of Us","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["african","all"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["birth","blessing","home"],"text":"This child belongs to all of us.\nTo the mother and father, the aunties and uncles,\nthe grandparents, the neighbors, and the friends.\nWe promise to love, teach, and protect this little one.\nWelcome home.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a baby welcoming or dedication, in the spirit of the whole community raising a child.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-african-elder","title":"For an Elder Gone Home","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["african","blackchurch"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["funeral","grief"],"text":"You have gone home to the ancestors.\nYou go as one who is honored.\nWe will remember your laughter and your counsel.\nWe will tell your story to the children.\nRest now, beloved elder. Your work is done, and it is good.","note":"A Grounded blessing for the funeral or homegoing of a beloved elder.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-african-harvest","title":"For the First Fruits","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["african","all"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["gratitude","season","celebration"],"text":"We give thanks for the harvest and the hands that brought it in.\nWe give thanks for family gathered and food shared.\nWe remember those who came before and those still to come.\nMay there be enough for all, and joy in every house.","note":"A Grounded gratitude blessing for harvest and first fruits gatherings, including Kwanzaa.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-african-wedding","title":"Two Families Become One","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["african","all"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["wedding","blessing"],"text":"Today two people join hands,\nand two families become one.\nMay the elders bless you. May the ancestors smile on you.\nMay your home be full of peace, laughter, and good counsel.\nMay your love grow deep roots and wide branches.","note":"A Grounded wedding blessing honoring the African understanding of marriage as the joining of families.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-pagan-winter-solstice","title":"For the Winter Solstice","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","hope","evening"],"text":"Tonight is the longest night.\nWe light a candle and wait together.\nFrom this night forward the light returns, a little more each day.\nMay we rest in the dark and rise with the sun.\nWelcome back, light.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Yule and winter solstice gatherings.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-pagan-imbolc","title":"For the First Stirring of Spring","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","hope","home"],"text":"Under the snow, the seeds are waking.\nThe days grow longer. The ewes give milk.\nWe tend the hearth fire and clear the house for spring.\nMay every good thing begin to grow.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Imbolc and Candlemas, early February.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-pagan-spring-equinox","title":"For the Spring Equinox","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","hope","celebration"],"text":"Day and night stand in balance.\nThe earth wakes green.\nWe plant seeds in the soil and in our hearts.\nMay what we plant today grow strong and bear good fruit.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Ostara and the spring equinox.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-pagan-may-day","title":"For May Morning","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","celebration","wedding"],"text":"The hawthorn blooms. The fields are bright.\nWe dance for joy and for the green and growing world.\nMay love bloom, may the land be fruitful,\nand may every heart be glad this morning.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Beltane and May Day, a lovely season for handfastings.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-pagan-summer-solstice","title":"For the Summer Solstice","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","gratitude","morning"],"text":"The sun stands highest. The day is long and golden.\nWe give thanks for warmth and light,\nfor long evenings and ripening fields.\nMay we carry this light within us through the turning year.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Litha and the summer solstice.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-pagan-first-harvest","title":"For the First Harvest","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","gratitude","home"],"text":"We bake the first bread of the new grain.\nWe give thanks for the fields, the sun, and the rain,\nfor the work of many hands.\nMay there be bread for every table.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Lammas and Lughnasadh, the first harvest in early August.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-pagan-autumn-equinox","title":"For the Autumn Equinox","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","gratitude","evening"],"text":"Day and night stand in balance once more.\nThe harvest is gathered. The leaves turn gold.\nWe give thanks for all this year has given\nand let go of what is finished.\nMay we rest well in the season to come.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Mabon and the autumn equinox.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-pagan-samhain","title":"For Remembering the Beloved Dead","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","grief","funeral"],"text":"The year turns toward the dark.\nWe set a place at the table for those we love who have gone before.\nWe speak their names. We tell their stories.\nLove is stronger than the years between us.\nWelcome, beloved ones, in memory.","note":"A Grounded remembrance for Samhain, when many earth-centered families honor their dead.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-pagan-full-moon","title":"For the Full Moon","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["evening","season","peace"],"text":"Bright moon, round and full,\nyou light the path for every traveler.\nWe give thanks for all that has come to fullness.\nMay we shine with gentle light for one another.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a full moon gathering or an evening walk.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-pagan-new-moon","title":"For the New Moon","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["evening","hope","season"],"text":"The moon is dark and new.\nA quiet beginning, a fresh page.\nWe set our hopes before us like seeds in the soil.\nMay they grow with the growing light.","note":"A Grounded blessing for setting intentions at the new moon.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-pagan-handfasting","title":"For a Handfasting","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["wedding","blessing"],"text":"By earth that holds you, may your love be steady.\nBy water that flows, may your love be gentle.\nBy fire that warms, may your love be bright.\nBy air that carries every word, may your love be true.\nAs these hands are bound, so may your lives be joined in joy.","note":"A Grounded blessing of the four elements for a handfasting or nature wedding.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-pagan-return-to-earth","title":"Return to the Earth","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["pagan","nature"],"denom":["wicca","druid"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["funeral","grief","nature"],"text":"From the earth you came, and to the earth you return.\nYou will be in the green of spring,\nthe gold of autumn, the rain and the wind.\nThe circle of life holds you.\nMerry meet, merry part, and merry meet again.","note":"A Grounded blessing for an earth-centered funeral or a green burial; it closes with a traditional Pagan farewell.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-shinto-purity","title":"For a Clear and Bright Heart","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["shinto"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["morning","daily","peace"],"text":"With fresh water we wash our hands and rinse our mouths.\nWe bow to the morning.\nMay our hearts be clear and bright,\nour words sincere,\nand our day full of gratitude.","note":"A Grounded morning blessing in the spirit of Shinto purity and sincerity (makoto).","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-shinto-nature","title":"For the Kami of River and Mountain","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["shinto","nature"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["nature","gratitude"],"text":"We give thanks for the mountain and the river,\nthe old tree and the quiet stone,\nthe sun that rises and the rice that grows.\nAll things are full of life and wonder.\nWe bow in gratitude.","note":"A Grounded blessing in the spirit of Shinto reverence for the sacred in nature.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-shinto-ancestors","title":"For the Family Ancestors","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["shinto","buddhist"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["home","grief","gratitude"],"text":"We bring fresh rice, fresh water, and flowers.\nWe light the candle and bow.\nThank you, honored ancestors, for watching over this house.\nWe will live well, and remember you always.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a family altar in Japanese homes, where Shinto and Buddhist remembrance meet.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-taoist-flow","title":"Like Water","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["taoist"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["peace","healing","daily"],"text":"Be like water.\nSoft, and strong enough to carve stone.\nLow, and so it gathers all the streams.\nStill, and so it shows the moon.\nMay you flow gently through this day.","note":"A Grounded blessing drawn from the water images of the Tao Te Ching.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-taoist-return","title":"Returning to the Root","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["taoist"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["funeral","dying","comfort"],"text":"The leaf returns to the root.\nThe river returns to the sea.\nThe breath returns to the great breath of all things.\nRest now in the stillness of the Way.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a memorial, inspired by chapter 16 of the Tao Te Ching.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-confucian-qingming","title":"For Tomb-Sweeping Day","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["confucian","taoist","buddhist"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","grief","gratitude"],"text":"We sweep the stones and bring fresh flowers.\nWe bow to our parents and grandparents.\nThe willows are green, the spring is new,\nand our love for you is green and new as well.\nWe remember. We give thanks.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Qingming, the spring festival of remembering the ancestors.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-confucian-filial","title":"For Honored Parents","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["confucian"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["home","gratitude","celebration"],"text":"Thank you for the rice you cooked and the lessons you gave.\nThank you for every early morning and late night.\nMay your years be long and peaceful.\nWe will honor you in life and remember you always.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a parent’s birthday or a family gathering honoring elders.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-lunar-new-year","title":"For the Lunar New Year","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["confucian","taoist","buddhist"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","celebration","home"],"text":"The old year is swept away. The new year is bright.\nMay this house be full of health and peace.\nMay the family be together and the table be full.\nMay every wish for good come true.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Lunar New Year, shared by Chinese, Vietnamese, and Korean families.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hindu-diwali","title":"For the Festival of Lights","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hindu","sikh","jain"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","celebration","home"],"text":"We light the lamps in every window.\nLight over darkness, wisdom over ignorance, good over harm.\nMay this house be bright with love\nand every heart be bright with hope.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Diwali, celebrated by Hindu, Sikh, and Jain families.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hindu-new-home","title":"For Entering a New Home","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hindu"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["home","blessing"],"text":"We step in with the right foot first.\nWe bring light, water, and sweet things.\nMay this house be peaceful and pure,\nfull of learning, plenty, and laughter.\nMay every guest be welcomed here as a gift.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a housewarming, offered alongside the family’s own griha pravesh puja.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hindu-new-baby","title":"For a Child’s First Days","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hindu"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["birth","blessing"],"text":"Welcome, little light.\nMay you grow in health and wisdom,\nin kindness and courage.\nMay your name bring you blessing,\nand may your life be a gift to the world.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a newborn, alongside the family’s naming ceremony.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-buddhist-merit","title":"Sharing Merit with the Departed","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["buddhist"],"denom":["theravada"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["funeral","grief","dying"],"text":"May the good of this day reach you, beloved one.\nAs the rivers fill the ocean,\nmay every kind deed done in your name\nflow to you and bring you peace.","note":"A Grounded blessing in the spirit of the Theravada practice of sharing merit with the dead, used in Thai, Lao, Khmer, Burmese, and Sri Lankan families.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-buddhist-metta","title":"May All Beings Be Happy","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["buddhist","all"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["daily","peace","blessing"],"text":"May I be happy. May I be well. May I be at peace.\nMay those I love be happy, well, and at peace.\nMay those I find difficult be happy, well, and at peace.\nMay all beings everywhere be happy, well, and at peace.","note":"A Grounded loving-kindness blessing in the widely used circles form, from self to all beings.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-vietnamese-altar","title":"For the Family Altar","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["buddhist","confucian"],"denom":["mahayana"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["home","grief","gratitude"],"text":"We light the incense and bow.\nGrandmother, grandfather, mother, father,\nyou are always with us.\nWe offer fruit and flowers and our thanks.\nWatch over the family. We will make you proud.","note":"A Grounded blessing for the ancestor altar kept in many Vietnamese homes.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-buddhist-hospital","title":"For the Sick, in the Buddha’s Spirit","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["buddhist"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["healing","comfort"],"text":"May your body find ease.\nMay your mind find calm.\nMay your heart rest in loving-kindness,\nheld by all who care for you.\nBreathing in, peace. Breathing out, peace.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a hospital visit, in the spirit of the Buddha who tended the sick with his own hands.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-stray-birds-grief","title":"Let Life Be Beautiful, from Stray Birds","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1916,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["grief","funeral","comfort"],"text":"If you shed tears when you miss the sun, you also miss the stars.\n\nLet life be beautiful like summer flowers and death like autumn leaves.\n\nThe raindrops kissed the earth and whispered, \"We are thy homesick children, mother, come back to thee from the heaven.\"\n\nIt is the little things that I leave behind for my loved ones, great things are for everyone.\n\nMy flower of the day dropped its petals forgotten. In the evening it ripens into a golden fruit of memory.\n\nLife has become richer by the love that has been lost.\n\nThis life is the crossing of a sea, where we meet in the same narrow ship. In death we reach the shore and go to our different worlds.","note":"Short sayings for grief, chosen from Stray Birds; each can stand alone on a card or program.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Stray Birds, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1916)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6524"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1916; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-stray-birds-birth","title":"Every Child Comes with the Message, from Stray Birds","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1916,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["birth","blessing","morning"],"text":"Every child comes with the message that God is not yet discouraged of man.\n\nThe infant flower opens its bud and cries, \"Dear World, please do not fade.\"\n\nDo not say, \"It is morning,\" and dismiss it with a name of yesterday. See it for the first time as a new-born child that has no name.\n\nThe world has opened its heart of light in the morning. Come out, my heart, with thy love to meet it.","note":"Short sayings to welcome a baby or bless a new morning.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Stray Birds, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1916)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6524"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1916; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-crescent-moon-beginning","title":"The Beginning, from The Crescent Moon","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1913,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["birth","blessing","celebration"],"text":"\"Where have I come from, where did you pick me up?\" the baby asked its mother.\n\nShe answered half crying, half laughing, and clasping the baby to her breast, \"You were hidden in my heart as its desire, my darling.\n\nYou were in the dolls of my childhood's games; and when with clay I made the image of my god every morning, I made and unmade you then.\n\nYou were enshrined with our household deity, in his worship I worshipped you.\n\nIn all my hopes and my loves, in my life, in the life of my mother you have lived.\n\nIn the lap of the deathless Spirit who rules our home you have been nursed for ages.\n\nWhen in girlhood my heart was opening its petals, you hovered as a fragrance about it.\n\nYour tender softness bloomed in my youthful limbs, like a glow in the sky before the sunrise.\n\nHeaven's first darling, twin-born with the morning light, you have floated down the stream of the world's life, and at last you have stranded on my heart.\n\nAs I gaze on your face, mystery overwhelms me; you who belong to all have become mine.\n\nFor fear of losing you I hold you tight to my breast. What magic has snared the world's treasure in these slender arms of mine?","note":"A mother answers her baby’s question; a tender reading for a birth or a baby blessing.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Crescent Moon, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1913)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6520"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-eastman-morning-sun","title":"Each Soul Must Meet the Morning Sun","by":"Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa), Dakota","year":1911,"trad":["indigenous"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["morning","daily"],"text":"In the life of the Indian there was only one inevitable duty, the duty of prayer, the daily recognition of the Unseen and Eternal. His daily devotions were more necessary to him than daily food. He wakes at daybreak, puts on his moccasins and steps down to the water’s edge. Here he throws handfuls of clear, cold water into his face, or plunges in bodily. After the bath, he stands erect before the advancing dawn, facing the sun as it dances upon the horizon, and offers his unspoken orison. His mate may precede or follow him in his devotions, but never accompanies him. Each soul must meet the morning sun, the new, sweet earth, and the Great Silence alone!","note":"Eastman describes the Dakota way of daily morning prayer at the water’s edge.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Soul of the Indian, Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa) (1911)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1911; Eastman died 1939"},{"id":"east-eastman-all-days","title":"All Days Are God’s","by":"Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa), Dakota","year":1911,"trad":["indigenous"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["nature","daily","gratitude"],"text":"Whenever, in the course of the daily hunt, the red hunter comes upon a scene that is strikingly beautiful or sublime, a black thundercloud with the rainbow’s glowing arch above the mountain; a white waterfall in the heart of a green gorge; a vast prairie tinged with the blood-red of sunset, he pauses for an instant in the attitude of worship. He sees no need for setting apart one day in seven as a holy day, since to him all days are God’s.","note":"A reading on pausing in wonder before the beauty of the land.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Soul of the Indian, Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa) (1911)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1911; Eastman died 1939"},{"id":"east-eastman-silence","title":"Silence Is the Cornerstone of Character","by":"Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa), Dakota","year":1911,"trad":["indigenous"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["peace","daily"],"text":"The first American mingled with his pride a singular humility. Spiritual arrogance was foreign to his nature and teaching. He never claimed that the power of articulate speech was proof of superiority over the dumb creation; on the other hand, it is to him a perilous gift. He believes profoundly in silence, the sign of a perfect equilibrium. Silence is the absolute poise or balance of body, mind, and spirit. The man who preserves his selfhood ever calm and unshaken by the storms of existence, not a leaf, as it were, astir on the tree; not a ripple upon the surface of shining pool, his, in the mind of the unlettered sage, is the ideal attitude and conduct of life.\n\nIf you ask him: “What is silence?” he will answer: “It is the Great Mystery!” “The holy silence is His voice!” If you ask: “What are the fruits of silence?” he will say: “They are self-control, true courage or endurance, patience, dignity, and reverence. Silence is the cornerstone of character.”","note":"Eastman on silence as the voice of the Great Mystery.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Soul of the Indian, Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa) (1911)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1911; Eastman died 1939"},{"id":"east-eastman-worship","title":"The Worship of the Great Mystery","by":"Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa), Dakota","year":1911,"trad":["indigenous"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["nature","peace"],"text":"The worship of the “Great Mystery” was silent, solitary, free from all self-seeking. It was silent, because all speech is of necessity feeble and imperfect; therefore the souls of my ancestors ascended to God in wordless adoration. It was solitary, because they believed that He is nearer to us in solitude, and there were no priests authorized to come between a man and his Maker. None might exhort or confess or in any way meddle with the religious experience of another. Among us all men were created sons of God and stood erect, as conscious of their divinity. Our faith might not be formulated in creeds, nor forced upon any who were unwilling to receive it; hence there was no preaching, proselyting, nor persecution, neither were there any scoffers or atheists.\n\nThere were no temples or shrines among us save those of nature. Being a natural man, the Indian was intensely poetical. He would deem it sacrilege to build a house for Him who may be met face to face in the mysterious, shadowy aisles of the primeval forest, or on the sunlit bosom of virgin prairies, upon dizzy spires and pinnacles of naked rock, and yonder in the jeweled vault of the night sky! He who enrobes Himself in filmy veils of cloud, there on the rim of the visible world where our Great-Grandfather Sun kindles his evening camp-fire, He who rides upon the rigorous wind of the north, or breathes forth His spirit upon aromatic southern airs, whose war-canoe is launched upon majestic rivers and inland seas, He needs no lesser cathedral!","note":"Eastman’s description of his people’s way of prayer, in nature and in silence.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Soul of the Indian, Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa) (1911)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1911; Eastman died 1939"},{"id":"east-eastman-death","title":"Simplicity and Perfect Calm","by":"Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa), Dakota","year":1911,"trad":["indigenous"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["dying","grief","funeral"],"text":"The attitude of the Indian toward death, the test and background of life, is entirely consistent with his character and philosophy. Death has no terrors for him; he meets it with simplicity and perfect calm, seeking only an honorable end as his last gift to his family and descendants. Therefore he courts death in battle; on the other hand, he would regard it as disgraceful to be killed in a private quarrel. If one be dying at home, it is customary to carry his bed out of doors as the end approaches, that his spirit may pass under the open sky.\n\nNext to this, the matter that concerns him most is the parting with his dear ones, especially if he have any little children who must be left behind to suffer want. His family affections are strong, and he grieves intensely for the lost, even though he has unbounded faith in a spiritual companionship.","note":"Eastman on meeting death with calm and on the deep love of family that grieves.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Soul of the Indian, Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa) (1911)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1911; Eastman died 1939"},{"id":"east-eastman-birth","title":"The Great Song of Creation","by":"Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa), Dakota","year":1911,"trad":["indigenous"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["birth","blessing"],"text":"The Indian was a religious man from his mother’s womb. From the moment of her recognition of the fact of conception to the end of the second year of life, which was the ordinary duration of lactation, it was supposed by us that the mother’s spiritual influence counted for most. Her attitude and secret meditations must be such as to instill into the receptive soul of the unborn child the love of the “Great Mystery” and a sense of brotherhood with all creation. Silence and isolation are the rule of life for the expectant mother. She wanders prayerful in the stillness of great woods, or on the bosom of the untrodden prairie, and to her poetic mind the immanent birth of her child prefigures the advent of a master-man, a hero, or the mother of heroes, a thought conceived in the virgin breast of primeval nature, and dreamed out in a hush that is only broken by the sighing of the pine tree or the thrilling orchestra of a distant waterfall.\n\nAnd when the day of days in her life dawns, the day in which there is to be a new life, the miracle of whose making has been intrusted to her, she seeks no human aid. She has been trained and prepared in body and mind for this her holiest duty, ever since she can remember. The ordeal is best met alone, where no curious or pitying eyes embarrass her; where all nature says to her spirit: “‘Tis love! ‘tis love! the fulfilling of life!” When a sacred voice comes to her out of the silence, and a pair of eyes open upon her in the wilderness, she knows with joy that she has borne well her part in the great song of creation!","note":"Eastman on the expectant mother’s prayerful time before birth.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Soul of the Indian, Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa) (1911)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1911; Eastman died 1939"},{"id":"east-eastman-grace","title":"Spirit, Partake!","by":"Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa), Dakota","year":1911,"trad":["indigenous"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["gratitude","home","daily"],"text":"When food is taken, the woman murmurs a “grace” as she lowers the kettle; an act so softly and unobtrusively performed that one who does not know the custom usually fails to catch the whisper: “Spirit, partake!” As her husband receives the bowl or plate, he likewise murmurs his invocation to the spirit.","note":"Eastman describes the whispered grace at the family meal.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Soul of the Indian, Charles Alexander Eastman (Ohiyesa) (1911)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1911; Eastman died 1939"},{"id":"east-zitkala-loving-mystery","title":"The Loving Mystery Round About Us","by":"Zitkala-Sa (Red Bird), Yankton Dakota","year":1921,"trad":["indigenous","nature"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["nature","peace","daily"],"text":"When the spirit swells my breast I love to roam leisurely among the green hills; or sometimes, sitting on the brink of the murmuring Missouri, I marvel at the great blue overhead. With half-closed eyes I watch the huge cloud shadows in their noiseless play upon the high bluffs opposite me, while into my ear ripple the sweet, soft cadences of the river's song. Folded hands lie in my lap, for the time forgot. My heart and I lie small upon the earth like a grain of throbbing sand. Drifting clouds and tinkling waters, together with the warmth of a genial summer day, bespeak with eloquence the loving Mystery round about us. During the idle while I sat upon the sunny river brink, I grew somewhat, though my response be not so clearly manifest as in the green grass fringing the edge of the high bluff back of me.","note":"Zitkala-Sa sits by the Missouri River and feels the loving Mystery all around.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"American Indian Stories, Zitkala-Sa (1921), first printed as Why I Am a Pagan (1902)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10376"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1902 and 1921; Zitkala-Sa died 1938"},{"id":"east-zitkala-flowers","title":"Living Symbols","by":"Zitkala-Sa (Red Bird), Yankton Dakota","year":1921,"trad":["indigenous","nature"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["nature","gratitude"],"text":"At length retracing the uncertain footpath scaling the precipitous embankment, I seek the level lands where grow the wild prairie flowers. And they, the lovely little folk, soothe my soul with their perfumed breath.\n\nTheir quaint round faces of varied hue convince the heart which leaps with glad surprise that they, too, are living symbols of omnipotent thought. With a child's eager eye I drink in the myriad star shapes wrought in luxuriant color upon the green. Beautiful is the spiritual essence they embody.","note":"Zitkala-Sa on wild prairie flowers as living symbols of the Creator.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"American Indian Stories, Zitkala-Sa (1921), first printed as Why I Am a Pagan (1902)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10376"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1902 and 1921; Zitkala-Sa died 1938"},{"id":"east-zitkala-all-akin","title":"All Are Akin","by":"Zitkala-Sa (Red Bird), Yankton Dakota","year":1921,"trad":["indigenous","nature"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["peace","daily"],"text":"Finally resuming the chair at my desk I feel in keen sympathy with my fellow-creatures, for I seem to see clearly again that all are akin. The racial lines, which once were bitterly real, now serve nothing more than marking out a living mosaic of human beings. And even here men of the same color are like the ivory keys of one instrument where each resembles all the rest, yet varies from them in pitch and quality of voice.","note":"A reading on the kinship of all people, like keys of one instrument.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"American Indian Stories, Zitkala-Sa (1921), first printed as Why I Am a Pagan (1902)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10376"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1902 and 1921; Zitkala-Sa died 1938"},{"id":"east-zitkala-royal-mantle","title":"The Fluttering Robe of the Great Spirit","by":"Zitkala-Sa (Red Bird), Yankton Dakota","year":1921,"trad":["indigenous","nature"],"denom":["dakota"],"type":"reflection","occasion":["nature","peace","funeral"],"text":"Here, in a fleeting quiet, I am awakened by the fluttering robe of the Great Spirit. To my innermost consciousness the phenomenal universe is a royal mantle, vibrating with His divine breath. Caught in its flowing fringes are the spangles and oscillating brilliants of sun, moon, and stars.","note":"The closing lines of her essay, read outdoors or at a memorial under the sky.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"American Indian Stories, Zitkala-Sa (1921), first printed as Why I Am a Pagan (1902)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10376"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1902 and 1921; Zitkala-Sa died 1938"},{"id":"east-norito-purification","title":"As the Many-Piled Clouds Are Scattered, from the Great Purification","by":"Traditional, the Engishiki norito","tr":"W. G. Aston","year":1905,"trad":["shinto"],"type":"prayer","occasion":["forgiveness","peace","healing"],"text":"As the many-piled clouds of Heaven are scattered by the breath of the Wind-Gods; as the morning breezes and the evening breezes dissipate the dense morning vapours and the dense evening vapours; as a huge ship, moored in a great harbour, casting off its stern moorings, casting off its bow moorings, drives forth into the great sea-plain; as yonder thick brushwood is smitten and cleared away by the sharp sickle forged in the fire, so shall all offences be utterly annulled. Therefore he (the Mikado) is graciously pleased to purify and cleanse them away. The Goddess called Se-ori-tsu-hime who dwells in the rapids of the swift streams whose cataracts tumble headlong from the tops of the high mountains and from the tops of the low mountains will bear them out into the great sea-plain. Thereupon the Goddess called Haya-aki-tsu-hime, who dwells in the myriad meetings of the tides of the myriad brine-paths of the myriad ways of the currents of the boisterous sea will swallow them up. And the God Ibuki-do nushi, who dwells in the Breath-blowing-place, will puff them away to the Root-country, the Bottom Country. Then the Goddess Haya-sasura-hime, who dwells in the Root-country, the Bottom-country, will banish and abolish them. When they have been so destroyed, every one, from the servants of the Imperial Court to the four quarters of the Under-Heaven, will remain void of all offences whatsoever.","note":"From the Oharae no Kotoba, the Great Purification prayer said at shrines in summer and winter to wash away all faults.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Shinto: The Way of the Gods, W. G. Aston (1905)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/46038"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1905; Aston died 1911","denom":[]},{"id":"east-shinto-common-prayer","title":"Peace to the Country, Safety to the Family","by":"Traditional Shinto prayer","tr":"W. G. Aston","year":1905,"trad":["shinto"],"type":"prayer","occasion":["home","blessing","daily"],"text":"Peace to the country, safety to the family, and plentiful crops.","note":"A common shrine prayer of ordinary families, as Aston recorded it in 1905.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Shinto: The Way of the Gods, W. G. Aston (1905)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/46038"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1905; Aston died 1911","denom":[]},{"id":"east-ptahhotep-fair-speech","title":"Fair Speech Is More Rare Than the Emerald","by":"Ptah-hotep","tr":"Battiscombe G. Gunn","year":1906,"trad":["african"],"denom":["kemetic"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["daily"],"text":"Be not proud because thou art learned; but discourse with the ignorant man, as with the sage. For no limit can be set to skill, neither is there any craftsman that possesseth full advantages. Fair speech is more rare than the emerald that is found by slave-maidens on the pebbles.","note":"From the oldest book of wisdom in the world, written in Egypt over four thousand years ago.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Instruction of Ptah-hotep, tr. Battiscombe G. Gunn (1906)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/30508"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1906; Gunn died 1950, US public domain by date"},{"id":"east-ptahhotep-kindliness","title":"Live in the House of Kindliness","by":"Ptah-hotep","tr":"Battiscombe G. Gunn","year":1906,"trad":["african"],"denom":["kemetic"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["home","daily"],"text":"It is another that attaineth by giving unto him that hath not; not he that causeth men dread. For it happeneth that what the God hath commanded, even that thing cometh to pass. Live, therefore, in the house of kindliness, and men shall come and give gifts of themselves.","note":"An ancient Egyptian teaching on generosity, fitting for a home blessing.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Instruction of Ptah-hotep, tr. Battiscombe G. Gunn (1906)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/30508"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1906; Gunn died 1950, US public domain by date"},{"id":"east-ptahhotep-follow-heart","title":"Follow Thine Heart During Thy Lifetime","by":"Ptah-hotep","tr":"Battiscombe G. Gunn","year":1906,"trad":["african"],"denom":["kemetic"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["daily","celebration"],"text":"Follow thine heart during thy lifetime; do not more than is commanded thee. Diminish not the time of following the heart; it is abhorred of the soul, that its time [of ease] be taken away. Shorten not the daytime more than is needful to maintain thine house. When riches are gained, follow the heart; for riches are of no avail if one be weary.","note":"An ancient call to rest and joy, read for retirements and milestones.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Instruction of Ptah-hotep, tr. Battiscombe G. Gunn (1906)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/30508"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1906; Gunn died 1950, US public domain by date"},{"id":"east-ptahhotep-love-wife","title":"Love Thy Wife That Is in Thine Arms","by":"Ptah-hotep","tr":"Battiscombe G. Gunn","year":1906,"trad":["african"],"denom":["kemetic"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["wedding","home"],"text":"If thou wouldest be wise, provide for thine house, and love thy wife that is in thine arms. Fill her stomach, clothe her back; oil is the remedy of her limbs. Gladden her heart during thy lifetime.","note":"Ancient Egyptian counsel for a loving marriage, often read at weddings honoring African heritage.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Instruction of Ptah-hotep, tr. Battiscombe G. Gunn (1906)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/30508"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1906; Gunn died 1950, US public domain by date"},{"id":"east-hymn-to-the-nile","title":"Hail to Thee, O Nile","by":"Traditional Egyptian hymn","tr":"F. C. Cook","year":1901,"trad":["african"],"denom":["kemetic"],"type":"hymn","occasion":["nature","gratitude","season"],"text":"Hail to thee O Nile!\nThou showest thyself in this land,\nComing in peace, giving life to Egypt:\nO Ammon, (thou) leadest night unto day,\nA leading that rejoices the heart!\nOverflowing the gardens created by Rā.\nGiving life to all animals;\nWatering the land without ceasing:\nThe way of heaven descending:\nLover of food, bestower of corn,\nGiving light to every home, O Ptah!","note":"An ancient hymn of thanks for the river that gives life, for harvest and gratitude gatherings.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Egyptian Literature, ed. Epiphanius Wilson (1901), Hymn to the Nile tr. F. C. Cook","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/28282"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1901; Cook died 1889"},{"id":"east-homeric-earth","title":"To Earth the Mother of All","by":"Traditional, the Homeric Hymns","tr":"Hugh G. Evelyn-White","year":1914,"trad":["pagan"],"denom":["hellenic"],"type":"hymn","occasion":["nature","gratitude","season"],"text":"I will sing of well-founded Earth, mother of all, eldest of all beings. She feeds all creatures that are in the world, all that go upon the goodly land, and all that are in the paths of the seas, and all that fly: all these are fed of her store. Through you, O queen, men are blessed in their children and blessed in their harvests, and to you it belongs to give means of life to mortal men and to take it away. Happy is the man whom you delight to honour! He has all things abundantly: his fruitful land is laden with corn, his pastures are covered with cattle, and his house is filled with good things. Such men rule orderly in their cities of fair women: great riches and wealth follow them: their sons exult with ever-fresh delight, and their daughters in flower-laden bands play and skip merrily over the soft flowers of the field. Thus is it with those whom you honour O holy goddess, bountiful spirit.","note":"An ancient Greek hymn to the Earth, loved in earth-centered circles for harvest and gratitude.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica, tr. Hugh G. Evelyn-White (1914)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/348"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1914; Evelyn-White died 1924"},{"id":"east-homeric-hestia","title":"To Hestia of the Hearth","by":"Traditional, the Homeric Hymns","tr":"Hugh G. Evelyn-White","year":1914,"trad":["pagan"],"denom":["hellenic"],"type":"hymn","occasion":["home","blessing"],"text":"Hestia, you who tend the holy house of the lord Apollo, the Far-shooter at goodly Pytho, with soft oil dripping ever from your locks, come now into this house, come, having one mind with Zeus the all-wise, draw near, and withal bestow grace upon my song.","note":"A short hymn to the keeper of the hearth, for blessing a home.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica, tr. Hugh G. Evelyn-White (1914)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/348"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1914; Evelyn-White died 1924"},{"id":"east-homeric-selene","title":"To Selene the Moon","by":"Traditional, the Homeric Hymns","tr":"Hugh G. Evelyn-White","year":1914,"trad":["pagan"],"denom":["hellenic"],"type":"hymn","occasion":["evening","season","nature"],"text":"And next, sweet voiced Muses, daughters of Zeus, well-skilled in song, tell of the long-winged Moon. From her immortal head a radiance is shown from heaven and embraces earth; and great is the beauty that ariseth from her shining light. The air, unlit before, glows with the light of her golden crown, and her rays beam clear, whensoever bright Selene having bathed her lovely body in the waters of Ocean, and donned her far-gleaming, shining team, drives on her long-maned horses at full speed, at eventime in the mid-month: then her great orbit is full and then her beams shine brightest as she increases. So she is a sure token and a sign to mortal men.\n\nHail, white-armed goddess, bright Selene, mild, bright-tressed queen!","note":"A hymn to the moon, for full moon gatherings and evening blessings.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica, tr. Hugh G. Evelyn-White (1914)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/348"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1914; Evelyn-White died 1924"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-6","title":"Pluck This Little Flower, Gitanjali 6","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"prayer","occasion":["dying","daily"],"text":"Pluck this little flower and take it, delay not! I fear lest it droop and drop into the dust.\n\nI may not find a place in thy garland, but honour it with a touch of pain from thy hand and pluck it. I fear lest the day end before I am aware, and the time of offering go by.\n\nThough its colour be not deep and its smell be faint, use this flower in thy service and pluck it while there is time.","note":"A prayer to be gathered while there is still time to offer something beautiful.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-16","title":"My Invitation to This World’s Festival, Gitanjali 16","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["gratitude","celebration","dying"],"text":"I have had my invitation to this world's festival, and thus my life has been blessed. My eyes have seen and my ears have heard.\n\nIt was my part at this feast to play upon my instrument, and I have done all I could.\n\nNow, I ask, has the time come at last when I may go in and see thy face and offer thee my silent salutation?","note":"A thankful look back on a life that was invited to the world’s festival.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-50","title":"I Had Gone A-Begging, Gitanjali 50","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"story","occasion":["gratitude","daily"],"text":"I had gone a-begging from door to door in the village path, when thy golden chariot appeared in the distance like a gorgeous dream and I wondered who was this King of all kings!\n\nMy hopes rose high and methought my evil days were at an end, and I stood waiting for alms to be given unasked and for wealth scattered on all sides in the dust.\n\nThe chariot stopped where I stood. Thy glance fell on me and thou camest down with a smile. I felt that the luck of my life had come at last. Then of a sudden thou didst hold out thy right hand and say 'What hast thou to give to me?'\n\nAh, what a kingly jest was it to open thy palm to a beggar to beg! I was confused and stood undecided, and then from my wallet I slowly took out the least little grain of corn and gave it to thee.\n\nBut how great my surprise when at the day's end I emptied my bag on the floor to find a least little gram of gold among the poor heap. I bitterly wept and wished that I had had the heart to give thee my all.","note":"A beloved parable poem: the grain of gold found in the giving.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-72","title":"He It Is, the Innermost One, Gitanjali 72","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["daily","peace"],"text":"He it is, the innermost one, who awakens my being with his deep hidden touches.\n\nHe it is who puts his enchantment upon these eyes and joyfully plays on the chords of my heart in varied cadence of pleasure and pain.\n\nHe it is who weaves the web of this maya in evanescent hues of gold and silver, blue and green, and lets peep out through the folds his feet, at whose touch I forget myself.\n\nDays come and ages pass, and it is ever he who moves my heart in many a name, in many a guise, in many a rapture of joy and of sorrow.","note":"A reading on the hidden presence that wakes us to life.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-76","title":"Day After Day, O Lord of My Life, Gitanjali 76","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"prayer","occasion":["daily","morning"],"text":"Day after day, O lord of my life, shall I stand before thee face to face. With folded hands, O lord of all worlds, shall I stand before thee face to face.\n\nUnder thy great sky in solitude and silence, with humble heart shall I stand before thee face to face.\n\nIn this laborious world of thine, tumultuous with toil and with struggle, among hurrying crowds shall I stand before thee face to face.\n\nAnd when my work shall be done in this world, O King of kings, alone and speechless shall I stand before thee face to face.","note":"A prayer to stand before God face to face, day after day.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-82","title":"Time Is Endless in Thy Hands, Gitanjali 82","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["peace","comfort","dying"],"text":"Time is endless in thy hands, my lord. There is none to count thy minutes.\n\nDays and nights pass and ages bloom and fade like flowers. Thou knowest how to wait.\n\nThy centuries follow each other perfecting a small wild flower.\n\nWe have no time to lose, and having no time we must scramble for a chances. We are too poor to be late.\n\nAnd thus it is that time goes by while I give it to every querulous man who claims it, and thine altar is empty of all offerings to the last.\n\nAt the end of the day I hasten in fear lest thy gate to be shut; but I find that yet there is time.","note":"A reading of patience and trust, for those who feel time running short.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-84","title":"The Pang of Separation, Gitanjali 84","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["grief","comfort"],"text":"It is the pang of separation that spreads throughout the world and gives birth to shapes innumerable in the infinite sky.\n\nIt is this sorrow of separation that gazes in silence all nights from star to star and becomes lyric among rustling leaves in rainy darkness of July.\n\nIt is this overspreading pain that deepens into loves and desires, into sufferings and joy in human homes; and this it is that ever melts and flows in songs through my poet's heart.","note":"Tagore on the ache of separation that runs through all the world and becomes song.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-gitanjali-99","title":"When I Give Up the Helm, Gitanjali 99","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1912,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["dying","comfort"],"text":"When I give up the helm I know that the time has come for thee to take it. What there is to do will be instantly done. Vain is this struggle.\n\nThen take away your hands and silently put up with your defeat, my heart, and think it your good fortune to sit perfectly still where you are placed.\n\nThese my lamps are blown out at every little puff of wind, and trying to light them I forget all else again and again.\n\nBut I shall be wise this time and wait in the dark, spreading my mat on the floor; and whenever it is thy pleasure, my lord, come silently and take thy seat here.","note":"A reading for letting go and letting the divine steer in the last days.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Gitanjali, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1912)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7164"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1912 to 1913; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-fruit-gathering-13","title":"To Move Is to Meet You, Fruit-Gathering 13","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1916,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["daily","hope"],"text":"To move is to meet you every moment, Fellow-traveller!\n\nIt is to sing to the falling of your feet.\n\nHe whom your breath touches does not glide by the shelter of the bank.\n\nHe spreads a reckless sail to the wind and rides the turbulent water.\n\nHe who throws his doors open and steps onward receives your greeting.\n\nHe does not stay to count his gain or to mourn his loss; his heart beats the drum for his march, for that is to march with you every step,\n\nFellow-traveller!","note":"A reading for the journey, for travelers and new beginnings.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Fruit-Gathering, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1916)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6522"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1916; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-fruit-gathering-49","title":"The Pain Was Great When the Strings Were Being Tuned, Fruit-Gathering 49","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1916,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["healing","hope","comfort"],"text":"The pain was great when the strings were being tuned, my Master!\n\nBegin your music, and let me forget the pain; let me feel in beauty what you had in your mind through those pitiless days.\n\nThe waning night lingers at my doors, let her take her leave in songs.\n\nPour your heart into my life strings, my Master, in tunes that descend from your stars.","note":"A reading for recovery: the pain of tuning gives way to music.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Fruit-Gathering, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1916)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6522"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1916; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-fruit-gathering-54","title":"The Cloud Said to Me, I Vanish, Fruit-Gathering 54","by":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1916,"trad":["hindu","all"],"denom":["brahmo"],"type":"poem","occasion":["grief","funeral","comfort","hope"],"text":"The Cloud said to me, \"I vanish\"; the Night said, \"I plunge into the fiery dawn.\"\n\nThe Pain said, \"I remain in deep silence as his footprint.\"\n\n\"I die into the fulness,\" said my life to me.\n\nThe Earth said, \"My lights kiss your thoughts every moment.\"\n\n\"The days pass,\" Love said, \"but I wait for you.\"\n\nDeath said, \"I ply the boat of your life across the sea.\"","note":"A short poem on how what seems to vanish lives on in another form.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Fruit-Gathering, Rabindranath Tagore, his own English (1916)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6522"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1916; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-kabir-73","title":"The Palanquin Came to Take Me Away, Kabir","by":"Kabir","tr":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1915,"trad":["hindu","sikh","sbnr"],"denom":["bhakti"],"type":"poem","occasion":["dying","funeral"],"text":"The palanquin came to take me away to my husband's home, and it sent through my heart a thrill of joy;\nBut the bearers have brought me into the lonely forest, where I have no one of my own.\nO bearers, I entreat you by your feet, wait but a moment longer: let me go back to my kinsmen and friends, and take my leave of them.\nThe servant Kabir sings: \"O Sadhu! finish your buying and selling, have done with your good and your bad: for there are no markets and no shops in the land to which you go.\"","translit":"ayau din gaune ka ho","note":"Kabir’s song of the soul as a bride carried to her Beloved’s home.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Songs of Kabir, tr. Rabindranath Tagore (1915)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6519"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1915; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-kabir-90","title":"To Whom Shall I Go to Learn About My Beloved?, Kabir","by":"Kabir","tr":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1915,"trad":["hindu","sikh","sbnr"],"denom":["bhakti"],"type":"poem","occasion":["daily","nature"],"text":"To whom shall I go to learn about my Beloved?\nKabir says: \"As you never may find the forest if you ignore the tree, so He may never be found in abstractions.\"","translit":"main ka se bujhaun","note":"A short saying: find the forest through the tree, and the divine through the near and real.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Songs of Kabir, tr. Rabindranath Tagore (1915)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6519"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1915; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-kabir-77","title":"Go Thou, My Swan, Kabir","by":"Kabir","tr":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1915,"trad":["hindu","sikh","sbnr"],"denom":["bhakti"],"type":"poem","occasion":["dying","funeral","comfort"],"text":"O my heart! let us go to that country where dwells the Beloved, the ravisher of my heart!\nThere Love is filling her pitcher from the well, yet she has no rope wherewith to draw water;\nThere the clouds do not cover the sky, yet the rain falls down in gentle showers:\nO bodiless one! do not sit on your doorstep; go forth and bathe yourself in that rain!\nThere it is ever moonlight and never dark; and who speaks of one sun only? that land is illuminate with the rays of a million suns.","translit":"cal hamsa wa des' jahan","note":"The soul as a swan flying to the land of the Beloved, read at the end of life.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Songs of Kabir, tr. Rabindranath Tagore (1915)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6519"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1915; Tagore died 1941"},{"id":"east-dh-lotus","title":"The Lily on a Heap of Rubbish, from the Dhammapada","by":"Traditional, the Dhammapada","tr":"F. Max Muller","year":1881,"trad":["buddhist"],"denom":["theravada"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["hope","healing"],"text":"As on a heap of rubbish cast upon the highway the lily will grow full of sweet perfume and delight, thus the disciple of the truly enlightened Buddha shines forth by his knowledge among those who are like rubbish, among the people that walk in darkness.","note":"The image of a lotus growing from rubbish by the road; a reading of hope in hard places.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Dhammapada, tr. F. Max Muller, Sacred Books of the East vol. 10 (1881)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2017"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1881; Muller died 1900"},{"id":"east-ttc-2","title":"The Work Is Done, Tao Te Ching 2","by":"Lao Tzu","tr":"James Legge","year":1891,"trad":["taoist"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["daily","celebration"],"text":"All in the world know the beauty of the beautiful, and in doing this they have (the idea of) what ugliness is; they all know the skill of the skilful, and in doing this they have (the idea of) what the want of skill is.\n\nSo it is that existence and non-existence give birth the one to (the idea of) the other; that difficulty and ease produce the one (the idea of) the other; that length and shortness fashion out the one the figure of the other; that (the ideas of) height and lowness arise from the contrast of the one with the other; that the musical notes and tones become harmonious through the relation of one with another; and that being before and behind give the idea of one following another.\n\nTherefore the sage manages affairs without doing anything, and conveys his instructions without the use of speech.\n\nAll things spring up, and there is not one which declines to show itself; they grow, and there is no claim made for their ownership; they go through their processes, and there is no expectation (of a reward for the results). The work is accomplished, and there is no resting in it (as an achievement).\n\nThe work is done, but how no one can see;\n'Tis this that makes the power not cease to be.","note":"On doing good without claiming it; fitting for honoring a humble life.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Tao Teh King, tr. James Legge, Sacred Books of the East vol. 39 (1891)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/216"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1891; Legge died 1897","denom":[]},{"id":"east-ttc-15","title":"The Skilful Masters of Old, Tao Te Ching 15","by":"Lao Tzu","tr":"James Legge","year":1891,"trad":["taoist"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["peace","daily"],"text":"The skilful masters (of the Tao) in old times, with a subtle and exquisite penetration, comprehended its mysteries, and were deep (also) so as to elude men's knowledge. As they were thus beyond men's knowledge, I will make an effort to describe of what sort they appeared to be.\n\nShrinking looked they like those who wade through a stream in winter; irresolute like those who are afraid of all around them; grave like a guest (in awe of his host); evanescent like ice that is melting away; unpretentious like wood that has not been fashioned into anything; vacant like a valley, and dull like muddy water.\n\nWho can (make) the muddy water (clear)? Let it be still, and it will gradually become clear. Who can secure the condition of rest? Let movement go on, and the condition of rest will gradually arise.\n\nThey who preserve this method of the Tao do not wish to be full (of themselves). It is through their not being full of themselves that they can afford to seem worn and not appear new and complete.","note":"A portrait of the wise: careful as one crossing a winter stream, clear as still water.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Tao Teh King, tr. James Legge, Sacred Books of the East vol. 39 (1891)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/216"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1891; Legge died 1897","denom":[]},{"id":"east-ttc-41","title":"Scholars of the Highest Class, Tao Te Ching 41","by":"Lao Tzu","tr":"James Legge","year":1891,"trad":["taoist"],"type":"scripture","occasion":["daily","hope"],"text":"Scholars of the highest class, when they hear about the Tao, earnestly carry it into practice. Scholars of the middle class, when they have heard about it, seem now to keep it and now to lose it. Scholars of the lowest class, when they have heard about it, laugh greatly at it. If it were not (thus) laughed at, it would not be fit to be the Tao.\n\nTherefore the sentence-makers have thus expressed themselves:\n\n'The Tao, when brightest seen, seems light to lack;\nWho progress in it makes, seems drawing back;\nIts even way is like a rugged track.\nIts highest virtue from the vale doth rise;\nIts greatest beauty seems to offend the eyes;\nAnd he has most whose lot the least supplies.\nIts firmest virtue seems but poor and low;\nIts solid truth seems change to undergo;\nIts largest square doth yet no corner show\nA vessel great, it is the slowest made;\nLoud is its sound, but never word it said;\nA semblance great, the shadow of a shade.'\n\nThe Tao is hidden, and has no name; but it is the Tao which is skilful at imparting (to all things what they need) and making them complete.","note":"A reading on the Tao that seems small and hidden yet completes all things.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"The Tao Teh King, tr. James Legge, Sacred Books of the East vol. 39 (1891)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/216"},"rights":"pd","pd":"published 1891; Legge died 1897","denom":[]},{"id":"east-songkran","title":"For the Water Festival","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["buddhist"],"denom":["theravada"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["season","celebration","home","gratitude"],"text":"Cool water poured gently over the hands of our elders.\nWe ask their blessing and give our thanks.\nMay the old year wash away and the new year begin fresh.\nMay every home be cool, peaceful, and happy.","note":"A Grounded blessing for Songkran, Pi Mai, and Chaul Chnam Thmey, the spring new year of Thai, Lao, and Khmer families.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded"},{"id":"east-shinto-new-baby","title":"For a Baby’s First Shrine Visit","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["shinto"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["birth","blessing"],"text":"Little one, today you meet the wide and wonderful world.\nMay you grow healthy and strong,\nprotected and loved,\ngrateful for the sun, the rain, and every good thing.","note":"A Grounded blessing for families taking a newborn on a first shrine visit (omiyamairi).","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-hmong-healing","title":"For Strength and Healing","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["hmong"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["healing","comfort"],"text":"May your spirit stay close and your body grow strong.\nMay your family surround you with care.\nMay the doctors' hands be skilled and the medicine be good.\nMay you come home soon to the people who love you.","note":"A Grounded blessing for a hospital visit with a Hmong family; families may also call their own shaman or elders. Words by Grow With Grounded.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-indigenous-morning","title":"For the New Day","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["indigenous","nature"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["morning","gratitude","daily"],"text":"Good morning, sun.\nGood morning, earth.\nThank you for this breath and this new day.\nMay I walk in beauty and kindness,\nand give more than I take.","note":"A Grounded morning blessing, written in its own words with respect for the morning prayer Eastman described.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]},{"id":"east-african-strength","title":"For Strength on the Road","by":"Grow With Grounded","year":2026,"trad":["african","blackchurch","all"],"type":"blessing","occasion":["hope","healing","daily"],"text":"You come from people who endured.\nYou come from people who sang in the hard places.\nTheir strength is in you.\nWalk on, lift your head, and keep going.\nYou are carried by many hands.","note":"A Grounded blessing of courage for hard seasons, honoring the strength of African and diaspora ancestors.","voice":"brian","source":{"name":"Grow With Grounded","url":"https://growwithgrounded.com/readings.html"},"rights":"grounded","pd":"written by Grounded","denom":[]}];
